::GEO_U04_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation changes size but preserves shape? {
=A. Dilation#A dilation scales lengths by a factor while preserving angle measures, so it supports similarity.
~B. Translation#A translation moves a figure without changing size.
~C. Reflection#A reflection flips a figure without changing size.
~D. Rotation#A rotation turns a figure without changing size.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A sequence says rotate, then dilate. What happens first? {
~A. The dilation#This reverses the stated sequence.
=B. The rotation#Transformation sequences are read in order unless notation states otherwise.
~C. Both at the same time#The sequence describes ordered steps.
~D. Neither transformation#Both transformations are part of the sequence.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A figure maps onto itself after a 180&deg; turn. What symmetry is shown? {
~A. Reflection symmetry#Reflection symmetry uses a mirror line, not a turn.
~B. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry slides a repeated figure.
=C. Rotation symmetry#Mapping onto itself after a turn is rotation symmetry.
~D. No symmetry#The figure maps onto itself, so symmetry is present.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have two pairs of congruent corresponding angles. Which criterion justifies similarity? {
~A. SSS congruence#The evidence is angle equality for similarity, not equal side lengths for congruence.
~B. HL congruence#HL applies to right-triangle congruence.
~C. CPCTC#CPCTC is used after congruence is proven.
=D. AA similarity#Two congruent angle pairs are enough to establish triangle similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Similar figures have corresponding sides 4 and 10. What is the scale factor from the first to the second? {
=A. 2.5#The scale factor is 10 divided by 4, which equals 2.5.
~B. 6#Subtracting side lengths does not give scale factor.
~C. 0.4#This is the reverse scale factor.
~D. 14#Adding side lengths does not describe similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio defines cosine for an acute angle in a right triangle? {
~A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is sine.
=B. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Cosine compares the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This is tangent.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This reverses cosine.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 5 and 12. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 17#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 7#Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
=C. 13#5<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 169, so the hypotenuse is 13.
~D. 60#Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A line divides a figure into matching mirror halves. What symmetry is shown? {
~A. Rotation symmetry#Rotation symmetry involves a turn.
~B. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry involves a slide.
~C. Dilation symmetry#Dilation changes size and is not the mirror relationship described.
=D. Reflection symmetry#A line of reflection creates matching mirror halves.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A model uses scale factor <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>. An actual length is 32 cm. What is the model length? {
=A. 8 cm#32 multiplied by <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> equals 8.
~B. 28 cm#Subtracting 4 does not use scale factor.
~C. 36 cm#Adding 4 does not use scale factor.
~D. 128 cm#This uses the reciprocal and scales up instead of down.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, opposite = 9 and adjacent = 12. What is tan A? {
~A. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub>#This reverses tangent.
=B. <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#Tangent is opposite over adjacent.
~C. <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>#This would use the hypotenuse and match sine if 15 is the hypotenuse.
~D. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>#This matches cosine if 15 is the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ramp rises 6 ft over a horizontal distance of 8 ft. What is the ramp length? {
~A. 14 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 2 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the ramp length.
=C. 10 ft#The ramp is the hypotenuse: 6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> = 100, so the length is 10 ft.
~D. 48 ft#Multiplying rise and run does not use the Pythagorean relationship.
}

::GEO_U04_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence supports SSS similarity? {
~A. One angle pair is congruent.#One angle pair alone is not enough.
~B. One side pair is congruent.#One side pair alone does not establish similarity.
~C. The triangles share a vertex.#Shared location is not a similarity criterion.
=D. All three pairs of corresponding sides are proportional.#SSS similarity requires all corresponding side ratios to be equal.
}

